    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "US Air Force plans 500 highly-autonomous collaborative combat aircraft by 2032, Defense One reports",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Defense One reports the US Air Force plans to field 500 highly-autonomous collaborative combat aircraft by 2032." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Air Force Secretary Troy Meink is quoted calling autonomy one of, if not the key technology enabling cost-effective combat power." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "There's a hard number on effectiveness too. Shield AI's president and co-founder Ryan Tseng told Defense One his company worked with Ukrainians to improve the strike capability of one of its drone weapons by 70 percent." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "So what's the constraint on getting there?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Trust, and the doctrine around it. Training, and defined permissions for autonomous decisions, rather than the airframes themselves. Michael Gregg of the Air Force Research Laboratory frames it as a trust and autonomy question." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Only one outlet carries this, and the Air Force isn't quoted giving a programme cost or a contract award, so treat the plan as reported rather than budgeted." }
      ]
    },
    {
